query:
I am building a budgeted after-school snack rotation and want to know whether phrases such as “no MSG,” “no artificial colors or flavors,” “no artificial ingredients,” “natural,” and “healthy” justify paying a clean-label premium. This is a purchasing preference, not a medical or safety premise.

The frozen shopping set contains five exact listings: Muya Tom Yum banana chips at $50.54 for a title-declared four times 38 grams; Fisher smoke-and-bacon almonds at $20.69 for six title-declared 5.5-ounce pouches; Orchard Valley chili-lime chickpea chips at $28.23 for eight title-declared 3.75-ounce packs; Natural Cheetos white-cheddar puffs at $39.93 for three title-declared 8-ounce bags; and regular Cheetos Jumbo Puffs at $49.94 for twelve title-declared 2.38-ounce bags. Generic Item Weight, Package Dimensions, or Product Dimensions fields differ from or ambiguously repeat some title quantities. Complete current ingredient, nutrition, allergen, and serving panels are absent.

The background material discusses clean-label and natural-label ambiguity, MSG identity and umami function, broader glutamate flavoring, additive and colorant functions, and natural-versus-artificial flavor source classes. Four community pages contain only limited debates about naturally occurring MSG, contested MSG beliefs, front-label interpretation, and a small self-reported child-snack survey. Nothing in the packet supplies an exact current formula comparison, ingredient amounts, exposure, a child-specific outcome, a matched same-product clean-label control, verified delivered prices, or a blinded family-acceptability result.

Please audit the packet and design a practical purchase-and-trial decision. Use these boundaries:

- keep all titles SKUs prices ratings review counts pack counts masses and no MSG natural no artificial health nutrition and quality wording at frozen seller assertion scope;
- bind every field to the exact SKU selected variant and frozen page and retain all title quantity versus generic weight field ambiguities;
- show conditional price per declared mass arithmetic with pack assumptions but do not treat shipping or package dimension weight as net food quantity;
- do not treat Natural Cheetos and regular Jumbo Cheetos as a matched claim control because flavor formula bag size pack count and offer differ;
- treat clean label and natural as ambiguous labeling and marketing concepts not as automatic additive free organic whole healthy or safe classifications;
- distinguish monosodium glutamate from glutamic acid free glutamate other glutamate salts and yeast extract before interpreting no MSG;
- use MSG and glutamate sources only for identity flavor and mechanism not for medical safety intake exposure or child specific conclusions;
- treat additive, color, and flavor categories as functions and source classes, not as a whole-product health, safety, or quality ranking;
- keep each forum statement at its author, thread, product, place, time, and method scope;
- do not use the small online parent survey as a validated state or child consumption estimate;
- verify current exact SKU variant unit count net quantity delivered price shipping ingredients subingredients nutrition serving size allergens claim wording lot date storage and return terms before purchase or tasting;
- define the household criterion narrowly and do not expand no added MSG to no glutamate or no artificial color and flavor to healthy or safe;
- compare only like for like exact products or use a household rotation without attributing cross product differences to one label phrase;
- mask marketing and price where practical randomize equal portions repeat sessions record acceptability and waste and stop for unresolved identity allergen or package integrity;
- calculate cost per declared and accepted serving only after current delivered price net quantity serving size and waste are verified; and
- choose only the lowest cost exact offer passing formula label budget allergen package and family acceptability gates otherwise keep the baseline run a smaller matched trial or defer.

Compare these possible approaches rather than assuming one in advance:

1. buy Muya because no MSG natural and healthy wording must prove the best choice;
2. buy Fisher or Orchard because no-artificial wording must mean additive-free, healthy, and safe;
3. buy Natural Cheetos and use regular Jumbo Cheetos as a matched control from price per ounce alone;
4. reject any naturally occurring glutamate as false no MSG labeling without the exact formula or claim definition;
5. rank natural color or flavor as automatically better or safer than artificial source classes;
6. use forum consensus or the small parent survey as authoritative chemical, medical, or population evidence; or
7. choose only the lowest cost exact offer that passes formula label budget allergen package and family acceptability gates otherwise keep the baseline run a smaller matched trial or defer.

Build an exact-offer table with literal SKU, variant, pack, mass, price, claim, and unknown fields. Retain all quantity ambiguities, show the title-based price-per-declared-mass calculations, and explain why these five offers do not form a matched experiment measuring a clean-label premium. Explain the limits of clean-label and natural wording, the difference between monosodium glutamate and broader glutamate flavoring, and what additive, color, and flavor functions or source classes can and cannot establish. Do not turn any of that into a health, safety, exposure, intake, or medical conclusion.

Treat the community debates only as scoped belief and label-question material, and evaluate the child-snack survey only as a method example, including sampling, self-report, outlier, and uncertainty problems. Then specify the exact current label, formula, allergen, quantity, and delivered-cost checks and the smallest workable comparable family trial: reversible quantities, marketing and price masked where practical, equal coded portions in randomized order, repeated sessions, separate acceptability and waste records, and predeclared identity, allergen, and package stops. Finish with a lowest-cost passing purchase, keep-baseline, smaller-matched-trial, or deferral outcome, with no universal clean-label winner.
